digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Investigate, develop, and evaluate vertically vibrated, wall-confined galloping bubbles as a tunable self-propelled system in which resonant vibration-mode interactions can break symmetry and enable controllable rectilinear, orbital, and run-and-tumble motion for bubble transport and manipulation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When bubble phenomena show counterintuitive motion, vibration-sensitive dynamics, mode-coupled self-propulsion, and practical value for bubble manipulation, they jointly motivate studying a controllable mechanism for programmable bubble transport and manipulation."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Given galloping self-propulsion driven by resonant interaction between bubble vibration modes and the knowledge that deformable bodies can move in inviscid fluids, deformation- or mode-coupled inertial-fluid effects are a plausible contributor to galloping-bubble propulsion without excluding other mechanisms."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Diverse, mechanism-driven bubble phenomena, together with cavitation collapse that can generate damaging shock waves, motivate pursuing controllable bubble actuation as a scientifically and practically valuable direction."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Given cavitation shock waves and energy focusing during gas-bubble collapse, bubble collapse is a plausible example of bubbles concentrating energy into extreme localized events such as shocks or light emission."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because actuated-bubble technologies are already technologically relevant across fields and bubble manipulation is valuable for removing bubbles in boiling-based cooling, controllable bubble actuation is a grounded opportunity for practical manipulation and removal tasks."];
  "n10" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given sinking bubbles in vertical vibration and knowing vibrations can invert/stabilize buoyancy-like effects, therefore a plausible mechanism is vibration-induced effective forces that can oppose buoyancy and strongly alter bubble migration."];
  "n11" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, downward-propagating bubble waves in carbonated drinks are observed and attributed to convection currents in the glass core."];
  "n12" [label="(7,15,3) Knowledge: Sinking bubbles/texture in stout beers is suggested to be due to a circulatory (convective) current in the glass."];
  "n13" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given downward bubble waves and knowing convection can drive downward near-wall motion, therefore a plausible explanation is that background flow fields (convection) can organize and transport bubble patterns counterintuitively."];
  "n14" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, under sudden pressure changes bubbles can collapse and generate damaging shock waves (cavitation)."];
  "n15" [label="(5,9,2) Knowledge: Energy focusing during gas-bubble collapse can lead to extreme events including light emission (sonoluminescence)."];
  "n18" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, actuated-bubble technologies exist and are used across numerous fields, implying controllable bubble actuation is technologically relevant."];
  "n19" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, manipulating bubble motion is valuable for boiling-based cooling because unremoved bubbles near heat sources reduce heat-transfer efficiency."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, rising bubbles can spontaneously abandon straight paths and trace helices/zigzags; this long-standing paradox indicates nontrivial path instability."];
  "n21" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, bubbles are exploited as cleaning tools (wastewater treatment, anti-biofouling, clearing bacteria), so controllable bubble motion can deliver practical surface/flow benefits."];
  "n22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, in medicine bubbles are used for cell mechanics interrogation and for drug/gene delivery, so controllable bubble actuation can be a biomedical tool."];
  "n25" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, a bubble in a vertically vibrated fluid chamber may spontaneously break symmetry and 'gallop' along the upper wall, self-propelled via resonant interaction between its vibration modes."];
  "n26" [label="(11,23,1) Knowledge: A deformable body can move persistently from rest through a perfect (inviscid) fluid."];
  "n28" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, adjusting vibrational forcing can tune galloping bubbles to switch among exploration modes: rectilinear, orbital, and run-and-tumble."];
  "n29" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, different bubble sizes and forcing conditions produce diverse domain exploration modes (observed experimentally)."];
  "n3" [label="(2,2,1) Knowledge: Path instabilities of rising (buoyancy-driven) bubbles are governed by fluid–body coupling and stability mechanisms."];
  "n30" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, a phase map shows bubble dynamics depend on driving acceleration and bubble volume, with detachment for small bubbles and breakup at large amplitudes."];
  "n31" [label="(0,0,0) Inductive commonality (general rule): If a system’s behavior changes systematically with control parameters (forcing amplitude/frequency, size/volume), then those parameters can be used to program and select desired dynamical regimes."];
  "n32" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given observed mode diversity and phase dependence on forcing and bubble size and a general rule about parameter control, therefore infer that galloping-bubble behavior is parameter-programmable and can be engineered for specific motion modes and constraints (e.g., avoid breakup/detachment)."];
  "n37" [label="(0,0,0) If diverse bubble phenomena indicate rich, mechanism-driven and sometimes counterintuitive dynamics, then developing a unified, controllable actuation mechanism for bubble motion is scientifically motivated and practically valuable."];
  "n4" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the observed helical/zigzag paths of rising bubbles and knowing that coupled fluid–body instabilities can generate path deviations, therefore a plausible explanation is that bubble motion can arise from an instability due to flow–bubble coupling."];
  "n5" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, under acoustic excitation bubbles can transition from in-place pulsations to erratic zigzag 'dancing' motion."];
  "n6" [label="(3,4,6) Knowledge: Bubble self-propulsion can be wholly accounted for by interactions of adjacent surface modes (n and n+1)."];
  "n7" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given acoustically driven zigzag 'dancing' and knowing mode–mode interactions can generate propulsion, therefore a plausible cause is resonant/phase-locked interaction between bubble vibration modes producing net motion."];
  "n8" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, in vertically oscillating baths bubbles may overcome buoyancy and sink, seemingly violating Archimedes’ principle."];
  "n9" [label="(6,10,6) Knowledge: Vibrations of liquids induce the paradoxical phenomenon of sinking air bubbles."];
  "n2" -> "n4" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
